NNAAMMEE
     ppkkgg__ddeelleettee — delete software package distributions

SSYYNNOOPPSSIISS
     ppkkgg__ddeelleettee [--aaccIIiimmnnqqssVVvvXXxx] [--BB _p_k_g_-_d_e_s_t_d_i_r] [--DD _n_a_m_e[=_v_a_l_u_e]]
                [_p_k_g_-_n_a_m_e _._._.]

DDEESSCCRRIIPPTTIIOONN
     The ppkkgg__ddeelleettee command is used to delete packages that have been previ‐
     ously installed with the pkg_add(1) command.

     Package names may be specified either as the package name itself, or as a
     filename which consists of the package name plus the “.tgz” suffix, or as
     a full pathname like _/_v_a_r_/_d_b_/_p_k_g_/_p_k_g_n_a_m_e, so that shell wildcards can be
     used.

     Version numbers and flavors (see packages-specs(7)) can be omitted, un‐
     less the resulting specification is ambiguous.

     Otherwise, _p_k_g_-_n_a_m_e in ppkkgg__ddeelleettee may use the same convenient shortcuts
     as pkg_add(1): ‘ppkkgg__ddeelleettee _a_u_t_o_c_o_n_f%_2_._1_3’ will delete _a_u_t_o_c_o_n_f from the
     _2_._1_3 branch.

     ppkkgg__ddeelleettee will syslog(3) deletions by default.

     ppkkgg__ddeelleettee relies on the file system information being consistent.  In
     case of a system crash, _/_v_a_r_/_d_b_/_p_k_g may become corrupted.  Use
     pkg_check(8) to repair things.

     ppkkgg__ddeelleettee may ask questions in interactive mode, or error out otherwise.
     Interactive mode is the default on a tty, see options --II//ii.

     The options are as follows:

     --aa      Delete unused dependencies (packages that are not needed by any‐
             thing tagged as installed manually).  Can be used without
             _p_k_g_n_a_m_e_s.  If used with _p_k_g_n_a_m_e_s, it will only delete non manual
             installs in the list.

     --BB _p_k_g_-_d_e_s_t_d_i_r
             Set _p_k_g_-_d_e_s_t_d_i_r as the prefix to prepend to any object deleted.

     --cc      Delete extra configuration files, users and groups mentioned as
             @@eexxttrraa, @@nneewwuusseerr and @@nneewwggrroouupp in the packing-list, respectively.

     --DD _n_a_m_e[=_v_a_l_u_e]
             Force removal of the package.  _n_a_m_e is a keyword that states what
             failsafe should be waived.  Recognized keywords include:

             bbaaddddeeppeenndd       Force the deletion of packages even if they ref‐
                             erence nonexistent dependencies.
             cchheecckkssuumm        Verify checksums before deleting files.
             ddeeppeennddeenncciieess    Also delete the whole set of packages that de‐
                             pends upon the requested packages.
             nnoonnrroooott         Uninstall even if not running as root.
             ssccrriippttss         External scripts may fail.

     --II      Force non-interactive mode.  Default is to be interactive when
             run from a tty.

     --ii      Force interactive mode, even if not run from a tty.  ppkkgg__ddeelleettee
             may ask questions to the user if faced with difficult decisions.

     --mm      Causes ppkkgg__ddeelleettee to always display the progress meter in cases
             it would not do so by default.

     --nn      Don't actually deinstall a package, just report the steps that
             would be taken if it were.

     --qqqq     Do not bother with checksums for configuration files.

     --ss      Don't actually deinstall packages, report the disk size changes
             that would happen.

     --VV      Turn on statistics output.  For now, only displays the number of
             packages done/total number of packages.  Several --VV will turn on
             more statistics in the future.

     --vv      Turn on verbose output.  Several --vv turn on more verbose output.
             By default, ppkkgg__ddeelleettee is almost completely silent, but it reacts
             to keyboard status requests (see stty(1)).  --vv turns on basic
             messages, --vvvv adds relevant system operations, --vvvvvv shows most
             internal computations apart from individual file/directory addi‐
             tions, and --vvvvvvvvvv shows everything.

     --XX      Delete everything, except the list of packages that follow.

     --xx      Disable progress meter.

TTEECCHHNNIICCAALL DDEETTAAIILLSS
     ppkkgg__ddeelleettee examines installed package records for the _p_k_g_n_a_m_e specified,
     checks inter-dependencies between installed packages, deletes the package
     contents in an order respecting dependencies (e.g., packages with depen‐
     dencies get removed first), and finally removes the package records.

     If a package is required by other installed packages not mentioned in the
     list of packages to remove, ppkkgg__ddeelleettee will list those dependent packages
     and refuse to delete the package.

